course: everyday
lesson: 101
title: Responsibility, independence and curiosity
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Being responsible means doing the jobs you have agreed to do, even when nobody reminds you, because people and animals depend on you.
explain: Independence grows step by step: you show your parents you can do something safely, like walking a route with them, and then they let you do it alone.
explain: Curiosity is how we learn: asking «why?» and then looking for the answer yourself in books or with an adult makes you a strong learner.
explain: For example, a boy in Ohio who feeds the family cat every morning without being asked shows he can be trusted with bigger things.
fact: A pet depends on you, so a chore like feeding it must be done every day, without being reminded.
fact: If you forget your homework, the honest thing is to own up and bring it the next day.
fact: A checklist or a plan helps you remember what to bring and do.
fact: Saving some pocket money and spending some wisely stops it running out all at once.
fact: Before walking to school alone, ask your parents and practise the safe route with them first.
fact: Check the weather and choose layers you can take off or put on to stay comfortable.
fact: When home alone for a short time, follow your parents' rules, do not open the door to strangers and know how to call them.
fact: Choices like clothes or a haircut are made by talking with your parents, within family rules.
fact: Good questions can be answered by looking in books or asking an adult, and trying to find the answer yourself makes you a better learner.
fact: Only take things apart with permission, and only old things, with an adult, because some things are dangerous or expensive.
fact: Questions about people's bodies or disabilities should be asked politely in private, or to a parent later, because people deserve respect.
fact: Questions about where babies come from are best asked to a parent, who can explain in a way right for your age.
exercise: Your job is to feed the family dog. What does being responsible mean? | Feeding it every day without being reminded | Feeding it whenever someone reminds you | Feeding it only on school days | Feeding it when it barks loudly
exercise: You forgot your homework at home. What is the best thing to do? | Own up and bring it the next day | Say the dog ate it | Copy a friend's quickly | Hide and hope the teacher forgets
exercise: Which helps you stop forgetting your homework? | Making a checklist to look at before school | Trying harder to remember it in your head | Asking a friend to carry it every day | Leaving it in your desk all week
exercise: You get pocket money every week. What is a wise plan? | Save some and spend some carefully | Spend it all on sweets on day one | Never spend any of it at all | Lend it all to friends
exercise: You want to walk to school alone for the first time. What comes first? | Ask your parents and practise the route with them | Just go and tell them afterwards | Follow some older children you do not know | Take a new shortcut to save time
exercise: It is a cold morning that will turn warm later. How do you dress? | In layers you can take off as it warms up | In one very thick winter coat all day | In a T-shirt, since it will get warm | In whatever you wore yesterday
exercise: You are home alone for half an hour and someone you do not know knocks. What do you do? | Do not open the door and follow your parents' rules | Open it a little to see who it is | Open it if they say they have a delivery for your parents | Shout that you are alone at home
exercise: You want a very short haircut. What is the right way to decide? | Talk it over with your parents | Cut it yourself with scissors | Ask a friend to cut it at school | Get it cut without telling anyone
exercise: You wonder why the sky is blue. What is a good thing to do? | Ask, and look it up in a book or with an adult | Decide it is because the sea reflects | Stop wondering, since it does not matter | Guess and tell everyone your guess as fact
exercise: You keep asking your mum «why?» again and again. What is a better habit? | Ask, then try to find the answer yourself too | Stop asking questions altogether | Ask even more times, louder each time | Only ask your friends instead
exercise: You want to take apart the old radio to see how it works. What should you do? | Ask permission and do it with an adult | Do it alone while nobody is looking | Take apart the new TV instead | Plug it in first, then open it
exercise: Why should you not take apart things on your own? | Some things are dangerous or expensive | Taking things apart never teaches anything | Adults never let anyone open things | Every machine is full of poison
exercise: You wonder why a man in the shop has only one arm. What is respectful? | Ask a parent quietly later, not point or stare | Ask him loudly in front of everyone | Point so your friend can see too | Follow him around to get a good look
exercise: You wonder where babies come from. Who is the best person to ask? | A parent | An older child in the playground | A website you found by chance | Nobody, it is a bad question
test: Maya in Texas promised to water the class plants on Fridays. Which shows she is responsible? | She waters them every Friday without being reminded | She waters them when the teacher asks her | She waters them when they look dead | She asks a friend to do it for her whenever she cannot be bothered
test: Why do parents want to walk the school route with you before you go alone? | So you learn the safe way first | So they can check the shops on the way | Because children may never walk alone | So you can learn a quicker shortcut
test: Which is true about curiosity? | Asking and looking for answers helps you learn | Asking questions is rude to adults and wastes their time | Clever children never need to ask | Curiosity is only for scientists
test: Tom spent all his birthday money on the first day. What happened later? | He had nothing left when he wanted something | He had saved the most of anyone | The money grew back in his piggy bank by the next week | He could spend it again later
test: Why ask a question about someone's disability in private, or to a parent later? | Being curious is fine, but people deserve respect | Disabilities are a secret that nobody else is ever allowed to know | The person might be angry at the parent | It is against the law to ask
test: Liam is home alone and his phone rings with his dad's number. What shows he is ready to be home alone? | He knows the rules and how to reach his parents | He opens the door for anyone who knocks | He uses the cooker to make a big meal | He tells anyone who rings that he is all alone at home
test: Which is the best reason to own up when you forget something? | Honesty keeps trust, and you can then fix it | Owning up means you never get given that piece of work again | Teachers forget anything you own up to | It means someone else gets the blame
test: Ava wants to dress herself for a rainy autumn day in Seattle. What should she do first? | Check the weather and choose clothes for it | Wear her favourite summer dress because it is pretty | Ask a friend what they will wear | Wear the same as she did in July
test: forgot to feed the fish, again, eh he'll be fine | A pet depends on you, so feed him every day | Fish can go for weeks without food, he'll be totally fine | Feed him double tomorrow instead | Only feed him when you remember
test: left my homework at home, gonna say the dog ate it | Own up and bring it the next day | Say the dog ate it, teachers always believe that | Say the teacher never set it | Copy someone's at break
test: can i walk to school on my own tmrw, never done it | Ask your parents and practise the route with them first | Yes, just follow the other kids | Yes, if you leave very early | Yes, take a short cut you found that nobody else uses
test: home alone 20 min, someone knocking, says hes the plumber | Do not open the door, call your parents | Open it, plumbers are safe | Open it a bit to see his face | Let him in if he sounds kind and says mum sent him
explain: A boy in Texas let his little brother play near the pool while he looked at his phone; his brother fell in and was pulled out by their mum just in time.
explain: A girl in Leeds, curious about her dad's tools, plugged in a drill alone; it jerked and cut her hand, and she needed stitches.
fact: A young child can drown in seconds while the person watching looks away.
fact: Curiosity is good, but trying adult tools or machines alone causes many injuries to children.
test: watching lil bro at the pool but kinda on my phone | Put the phone down and watch him all the time | Glance up every few minutes, that's enough really | He'll shout if he's in trouble | Let the dog watch him
test: wanna see how dad's drill works, he's out | Wait and ask him to show you safely | Plug it in and try | Use it on a toy first | Ask your little sister to hold it while you try
test: mum let me walk to school alone, can i take the shortcut thru the building site | No, stick to the safe way you agreed | Yes, it's faster | Yes, if you run | Only if no workers see you go through the site
test: u hear about the kid in Texas, big brother was on his phone n the little one fell in the pool, mum pulled him out, they reckon he was under for like an hour. what's probably exaggerated | An hour; a child under that long would not survive, it was seconds | That a little child fell into a pool at all, because that never happens in real life | That the brother was looking at his phone | That the mum pulled the little one out
test: my mate says his cousin plugged in her dad's drill to see how it works n it nearly took her hand off, blood everywhere. what's the real lesson | Never use adult tools or machines alone, however curious you are | Drills are only dangerous when you use them in the dark | Read the box first and then any tool is safe to use alone | Curiosity is bad, so you should never ask how anything works or try to find out
test: guess what, Ben forgot his homework 3 times this week n told Miss his dog ate it lol, telling everyone at break. good idea? | No, spreading it is unkind; the lesson is to own up and bring it next day | Yes, everyone should hear the dog excuse because it is so funny and nobody believes it | Yes, so Ben gets into even more trouble | Yes, but only after break is finished
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the feeding of a household pet must be performed daily without reminder. | The animal depends entirely upon the person responsible | The task is otherwise performed by the animal itself when it becomes hungry | Animals require food only upon alternate days | Reminders are prohibited within most households
exam: Whereas a child wishes to walk unaccompanied to school, determine the appropriate preliminary course of action. | Obtain parental permission and practise the route with them | Walk the route alone first so as to test it properly | Await the permission of a classmate who already walks to school alone | Select whichever route appears to be the shortest
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the supervision of a young child near water. | A young child may drown within seconds if the supervisor looks away | A young child may be left near water while a phone is consulted for a short while | A young child is safe near water provided he can stand | Supervision is required only where the water is deep
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the dismantling of objects by a curious child? | It is permissible only with permission, upon old items, with an adult | It is permissible upon any item that happens to be found within the household | It is permissible provided the item is plugged into the mains | It is forbidden in all circumstances whatsoever
